  "constraints": "* Input arrays/tensors are non-empty and well-formed\n* Floating point values are finite real numbers\n* Numerical operations must adhere to standard precision tolerances",
  "starterCode": "-- SQL Running Totals\nSELECT account_id, txn_date, txn_id, amount,\n       ROUND(SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY account_id ORDER BY txn_date, txn_id), 2) AS running_total\nFROM transactions\nORDER BY account_id, txn_date, txn_id;",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Running Totals** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\ndef sql_running_totals(transactions: list) -> list:\n    parts = {}\n    for t in transactions: parts.setdefault(t['account_id'], []).append(t)\n    res = []\n    for acc, txns in sorted(parts.items()):\n        cur = 0.0\n        for it in sorted(txns, key=lambda x: (x['txn_date'], x['txn_id'])):\n            cur += float(it['amount'])\n            res.append({'account_id': it['account_id'], 'txn_date': it['txn_date'], 'txn_id': it['txn_id'], 'amount': float(it['amount']), 'running_total': round(cur, 2)})\n    return res\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
